Slow down. There are quite some misunderstanding in here. First, OO is not
only a design concept, but also a comprehension concept - the latter even
taught in Kindergarden, as I've been told. If you can analyse problems or
tasks in OO terms - but note that not all problems and tasks are best
described in OO terms - then you'll find OO designs as most appropriate,
and OO languages best suited to support coding of those solutions. Second,
decent programmers are not available in abundance, rather quite rare if
you ask me. I don't consider C to be an inherent Bad Language; C has its
place. But languages are designed to support specific paradigms, and C is
no OO language; so, whenever you attempt to implement a basic OO concept
like polymorphism in such a language, even if folks try to implement it
decently, you'll see all of the non-application-domain glue that should
be hidden from the programmer. Finally, even with OO languages there's no
free meal; ability to program decently is required in this domain as well.

